
"smart_sources:2024-05-07 - One of Idaho’s most significant historic buildings is about to get a renovation.md": {"path":"2024-05-07 - One of Idaho’s most significant historic buildings is about to get a renovation.md","last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"last_read":{"hash":"ls0hjx","at":1785795029498},"class_name":"SmartSource","last_import":{"mtime":1785511718937,"size":19790,"at":1785795029740,"hash":"ls0hjx"},"blocks":{"#---frontmatter---":[1,9],"#":[10,45],"###What about those bars on the windows?":[46,61],"###What about those bars on the windows?#{1}":[48,61],"###An ongoing renovation project":[62,71],"###An ongoing renovation project#{1}":[64,71],"###Building’s history dates to gold rush":[72,113],"###Building’s history dates to gold rush#{1}":[74,113],"###The original investment? $75,000 from Congress":[114,129],"###The original investment? $75,000 from Congress#{1}":[116,129],"###A gold and silver exchange in Boise":[130,159],"###A gold and silver exchange in Boise#{1}":[132,159],"###Take the survey":[160,171],"###Take the survey#{1}":[162,171],"###Other Idaho landmarks":[172,192],"###Other Idaho landmarks#{1}":[174,192]},"outlinks":[{"title":"Scott McIntosh","target":"Scott McIntosh","line":5},{"title":"Idaho","target":"Idaho","line":14},{"title":"Dan Everhart, Idaho State Historic Preservation Office outreach historian, explains the damage that’s been done to the walls in the basement of the Assay Office building, 210 W. Main St., Boise. A $1.5 million project will repair and protect the walls and remodel parts of the basement for staff use.","target":"https://www.idahostatesman.com/public/latest-news/973f8c/picture288291635/alternates/FREE_1200/IMG_1387%20(1","line":32,"embedded":true},{"title":"This photo of the U.S. Assay Office, 210 W. Main St., Boise, from around 1900 shows the building without bars on the first-floor windows. The Idaho State Historical Preservation Office will remove the bars as part of a renovation project. Bars on the windows were not original to the building and are believed to have been added sometime around the beginning of the 1900s.","target":"https://www.idahostatesman.com/public/latest-news/68zmgv/picture288356890/alternates/FREE_1200/Assay%20Office%20and%20Grounds%20About%201900%20-%2063-60-1.jpg","line":42,"embedded":true},{"title":"This photo of the U.S. Assay Office, 210 W. Main St., from around 1910 shows bars on the first-floor windows, not believed to be original to the building, which was built in 1871. In an upcoming renovation project, the bars will be removed.","target":"https://www.idahostatesman.com/public/latest-news/n9x3wo/picture288357420/alternates/FREE_1200/Assay%20Office%20and%20Grounds%20About%201910%20-%20P1974-52-7%20copy.jpg","line":58,"embedded":true},{"title":"Save America’s Treasures","target":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/historicpreservationfund/save-americas-treasures-grants.htm","line":70},{"title":"At the peak of the second story of the Assay Office building, 210 W. Main St. in Boise, is a carved logo of the U.S. Treasury Department, which built the building in 1871 to be used as an assay office, processing gold and silver from 1872-1932.","target":"https://www.idahostatesman.com/public/latest-news/sqgocf/picture288295445/alternates/FREE_1200/IMG_1471.jpg","line":84,"embedded":true},{"title":"Just above the front door of the Assay Office building sits a carving of a six-petal flower, the ancient symbol for gold, a nod to the building’s first use as a processor of gold and silver.","target":"https://www.idahostatesman.com/public/latest-news/mzq4gb/picture288291645/alternates/FREE_1200/IMG_1372%20(1","line":92,"embedded":true},{"title":"Dan Everhart, Idaho State Historic Preservation Office outreach historian, explains the old coal-fired boiler system installed in the basement of the Assay Office in Boise by the U.S. Forest Service when it took over the building in 1932. The building is now connected to the city’s geothermal system, but the boiler remains.","target":"https://www.idahostatesman.com/public/latest-news/6mnc0r/picture288291680/alternates/FREE_1200/IMG_1382%20(1","line":110,"embedded":true},{"title":"https://history.idaho.gov/shpo/","target":"https://history.idaho.gov/shpo/","line":164},{"title":"![Profile Image of Scott McIntosh","target":"https://www.idahostatesman.com/public/latest-news/32oljv/picture234187782/alternates/FREE_1200/0813mcintosh02%20Cropped.jpg","line":180},{"title":"208-377-6202","target":"tel:208-377-6202","line":186},{"title":"Support my work with a digital subscription","target":"https://mycheckout.idahostatesman.com/subscribe?ofrgp_id=394&g2i_or_o=Event&g2i_or_p=Reporter&cid=news_cta_0.99-1mo-15.99-on-article_202404","line":188}],"metadata":{"title":"One of Idaho’s most significant historic buildings is about to get a renovation","source":"https://www.idahostatesman.com/opinion/from-the-opinion-editor/article288291170.html","author":["[[Scott McIntosh]]"],"published":"2024-05-07","created":"2026-07-31","description":"This building in Boise is on the National Register of Historic Landmarks.","tags":["#idaho"]},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[]},